"ui.17": "Traffic",
"autoroute.h1": "Driving on motorways: rules and good habits",
"autoroute.intro": "A motorway is a dual carriageway with no junctions, reserved for certain vehicles. Mistakes are rare there but serious, because everything happens fast.",
"ui.5": "Practise with a mock exam",
"ui.6": "All sheets",
"depassement.c0.h": "1. Conditions and steps",
"depassement.c0.b": "<p>Before overtaking, check that you have <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">enough visibility and distance</span>, that nobody is overtaking you and that the road ahead is clear for long enough. Then:</p><ul><li>check your mirrors and blind spot;</li><li>switch on the left indicator, then pull out;</li><li>overtake without dawdling and without exceeding the speed limit;</li><li>switch on the right indicator and pull back in when you can see <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">the overtaken vehicle in your rear-view mirror</span>.</li></ul><p>The driver being overtaken must not speed up. If there is oncoming traffic and no room, pull back in as soon as possible.</p>",
"depassement.c1.h": "2. When it is forbidden",
"depassement.c1.b": "<p>Overtaking is forbidden:</p><ul><li>by crossing a solid line;</li><li>on a bend or at the top of a hill when you cannot see far enough;</li><li>when approaching a level crossing;</li><li>when approaching a junction where you do not have priority;</li><li>when approaching a pedestrian crossing, and above all behind a vehicle stopped to let a pedestrian cross (the pedestrian may be hidden);</li><li>where a no-overtaking sign is posted.</li></ul><p class=\"fiche-note\">You normally overtake on the left. Overtaking on the right is allowed only in particular cases (a vehicle about to turn left, traffic in lanes).</p>",
"depassement.c2.h": "3. Cyclists, pedestrians, two-wheelers",
"depassement.c2.b": "<p>When overtaking a cyclist, pedestrian, horse rider or personal mobility device, leave a lateral distance of at least <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">1 metre in built-up areas</span> and <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">1.5 metres outside built-up areas</span>. If that is impossible, wait, even if it means slowing down. Do not sound your horn to frighten a horse or a cyclist.</p>",
