"stationnement.h1": "Stopping and parking: what is allowed and forbidden",
"intersections-ronds-points.intro": "A large part of the exam questions come down to the same question: who goes first? Here is the order to remember.",
"conduite-nuit-mauvais-temps.c0.h": "1. Lights, by night and by day",
"conduite-nuit-mauvais-temps.c0.b": "<p>At night, and whenever visibility is insufficient, switch on your <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">dipped headlights</span>. Use full beams outside built-up areas on an unlit road, but switch back to dipped as soon as you meet or closely follow another vehicle.</p><p><span class=\"fiche-highlight\">Fog lights</span> are only for fog or snowfall, not simply because it is raining. Side lights alone are not enough to drive: they are for being seen when parked. In tunnels, use dipped headlights.</p>",
"conduite-nuit-mauvais-temps.c1.h": "2. Rain, fog, snow and ice",
"conduite-nuit-mauvais-temps.c1.b": "<p>On a wet road, braking distance <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">can double</span>. Slow down and leave more distance to the vehicle in front. On motorways, the limit goes from 130 to 110 km/h. In fog with less than 50 metres of visibility, drive at 50 km/h at most.</p><ul><li>Aquaplaning: lift off the accelerator, hold the wheel firmly, do not brake hard.</li><li>Ice, snow: drive slowly, avoid sudden braking and acceleration.</li><li>In some mountain areas, winter equipment (chains or winter tyres) is compulsory from 1 November to 31 March.</li><li>Keep tyres in good condition: at least 1.6 mm of tread depth.</li></ul>",
"conduite-nuit-mauvais-temps.c2.h": "3. Glare and fatigue",
"conduite-nuit-mauvais-temps.c2.b": "<p>If you are dazzled by oncoming headlights, <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">slow down and look at the right-hand edge of the road</span>, without answering with full beams. At night, fatigue arrives faster: stop at the first signs (yawning, stinging eyes, heavy eyelids) and take a break of at least 15 to 20 minutes every 2 hours.</p>",
