"partager-la-route.q4.t": "Cyclist",
"partager-la-route.q4.q": "To overtake a cyclist outside built-up areas, the side margin is at least:",
"partager-la-route.f0.q": "What does a white cane mean?",
"partager-la-route.f0.a": "The person is blind or visually impaired. They have priority at the crossing like any pedestrian, and you give them time to cross.",
"partager-la-route.f1.q": "Why are motorcycles so hard to see?",
"partager-la-route.f1.a": "They are narrow and can hide in the blind spot or behind a pillar. Look twice before turning or changing lane.",
"partager-la-route.f2.q": "Can I park in a disabled space for just two minutes?",
"partager-la-route.f2.a": "No: it is very obstructive parking, punished by a €135 fine and the risk of towing.",
"stationnement.c3.h": "4. Blue zones and the parking disc",
"stationnement.c3.b": "<p>A <strong>blue zone</strong> is an area where parking is <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">free but time-limited</span>. It is shown by a sign (a white P on a blue background with a disc), and a plate gives the duration, days and hours.</p><ul><li>Place the <strong>European parking disc</strong> behind the windscreen, on the kerb side, clearly readable from outside.</li><li>Set it to your <strong>arrival time</strong>, rounded up to the next half hour, and do not change it while you stay parked.</li><li>The maximum duration is set by the town (often 1 h 30 or 2 h). Moving to another space in the same zone to extend your stay is forbidden.</li><li>Outside the days and hours shown, you can usually park freely, without a disc.</li><li>No disc, a wrongly set disc or overstaying: <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">€35 fine</span>.</li><li>Disabled people holding a parking card can usually park without a disc, subject to local rules.</li></ul>",
"intersections-ronds-points.c3.h": "4. Multi-lane roundabouts",
"intersections-ronds-points.c3.b": "<ul><li>At the entry, you <strong>give way</strong> to vehicles already in the circle, and you choose your lane on the approach by reading the signs and the road arrows.</li><li>Unless indicated otherwise: <strong>right-hand lane</strong> for the first exit (turning right), <strong>left-hand lane</strong> for turning left or making a U-turn. To go straight on, both lanes are possible.</li><li>In the circle, you keep your lane. To exit from the left-hand lane, you put on the <strong>right indicator</strong> just before the exit, check the mirror and blind spot, then move over.</li><li>The driver who <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">changes lane</span> must give way.</li><li>Never stop in the circle, and give way to pedestrians on the crossings at the exit.</li></ul>",
"stationnement.c4.h": "5. Stopping or parking: what is the difference?",
"stationnement.c4.b": "<ul><li><strong>Stopping</strong> is a <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">short</span> immobilisation: picking up or dropping off people, loading or unloading. The driver stays on board or right nearby and can leave immediately.</li><li><strong>Parking</strong> is any other immobilisation, especially when the driver leaves the vehicle or goes away.</li><li>A <em>no-stopping</em> rule (for example along a continuous yellow line) is stricter than a <em>no-parking</em> rule: you cannot even stop for a moment.</li><li>More than <strong>7 days</strong> in the same place is abusive parking (€35, towing possible).</li></ul>",
"stationnement.c5.h": "6. Spaces reserved for disabled people",
"stationnement.c5.b": "<ul><li>They are marked with the <strong>wheelchair symbol</strong> (sign and road marking).</li><li>Only holders of the <span class=\"fiche-highlight\">mobility inclusion card (CMI) with a “parking” mention</span>, clearly displayed behind the windscreen, may park there.</li><li>They are wider so doors can open fully and a wheelchair can be taken out.</li><li>Parking there without the right: <strong>€135</strong> fine and risk of towing (very obstructive parking).</li></ul>",
